Add({
	id = "uldaman",
	title = "Uldaman",
	aliases = { "the vault of Uldaman", "Uldaman dig site" },
	parent = "badlands",
	faction = "Contested",
	continent = "Eastern Kingdoms",
	zone = "Uldaman",
	unlock = {
		{ type = "zone" },
	},
	regionKind = "dungeon",
	kind = "region",
	sections = {
		{
			title = "Summary",
			text = [=[Uldaman is a vault of the titans buried beneath the northern {link:badlands}Badlands{/link}. It is older than any kingdom of the dwarves, older than the thrones of the elves, and perhaps as old as the shaping of the world. Its halls are cut from colossal blocks of stone and its doors are sealed with devices that no living smith can explain. For ages it lay hidden under the earth. Then the {link:explorers-league}Explorers' League{/link} of {link:ironforge}Ironforge{/link} dug its way in, and what the dwarves found there has shaken them to the root. Deep in the vault lie the {link:lore-keeper-of-norgannon}Discs of Norgannon{/link}, great platinum records that tell how the titans made the earthen from stone, and how the earthen became the {link:dwarves}dwarves{/link}.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "Origins",
			text = [=[No chronicle records when Uldaman was built. The records of the vault speak of the titans only as the Creators, and they say that the Creators shaped this world by their own design. Uldaman was one of their works. The earthen, beings of living rock, were made to labor in the deep places of the world, and the vault was a place where the knowledge of their making was kept.

Two accounts of that making are now told in {link:ironforge}Ironforge{/link}. The older legend says that the titans made the {link:troggs}troggs{/link} from stone first, judged them a failure, locked them away beneath the earth, and tried again, and that the second attempt gave rise to the dwarves. The discs themselves tell a different story, set down below. In both tales the troggs and the dwarves are kin, and in both tales Uldaman is where they were kept.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "The Present Day",
			text = [=[The dwarves of {link:ironforge}Ironforge{/link} once held the dig site at Uldaman. Their excavations broke into the forgotten city, and the troggs who slept in its depths awoke. They came up so fast and in such numbers that the diggers could not stand against them. Many dwarves were slain. The survivors fled, leaving their tools, their supplies and even their own chests behind in the common halls. A small band under the {link:dwarves}dwarf{/link} {link:the-lost-dwarves}Baelog{/link} had gone deep into the site just before the attack, and it was cut off behind the troggs' lines.

The troggs of the Stonevault clan now swarm through the vault under their chieftain {link:grimlok}Grimlok{/link}. They are not alone. The Shadowforge clan of the {link:dark-iron-dwarves}Dark Irons{/link} has sent great parties into Uldaman from its stronghold at {link:angor-fortress}Angor Fortress{/link}. Its diggers hunt for power stones and for the magic that gives life to stone, and it is said that their true prize is the discs, which they would claim for their fiery master, {red:ragnaros}Ragnaros{/red}. {link:royal-historian-archesonus}Royal Historian Archesonus{/link} counts these incursions among the signs that the Dark Irons are stirring as never before. Near the entrance the Shadowforge seized the explorer {red:hammertoe-grez}Hammertoe Grez{/red}, who had worked with {link:historian-karnik}Historian Karnik{/link} to find the lost artifacts of the site, and they killed him for what he knew.

Uldaman is not defenseless. Guardians of living stone walk its lower halls, and the earthen still labor in its deepest reaches. At the bottom of the city the {link:lore-keeper-of-norgannon}Discs of Norgannon{/link} are watched over by {link:archaedas}Archaedas{/link}, a giant of stone who sleeps until the vault is disturbed.

Many hands reach for the treasures of Uldaman. {link:advisor-belgrum}Advisor Belgrum{/link} of Ironforge wants the Tablet of Will, a shard that the titans are said to have inscribed with the power to give their creations a will of their own. {link:prospector-ironband}Prospector Ironband{/link} wants the carved urns that the late Prospector Agmond found outside the vault, for he suspects that such relics drive the troggs to fury. {link:prospector-stormpike}Prospector Stormpike{/link} wants word of Baelog. {link:theldurin-the-lost}Theldurin the Lost{/link} wants a tablet hidden in a chest older than any dwarven craft, which he believes holds the key to a doom he dares not name. The {link:gnomes}gnome{/link} {link:rigglefuzz}Rigglefuzz{/link} wants the power stones that the Shadowforge dig from the ruins. Alchemists of {link:thelsamar}Thelsamar{/link} and {link:kargath}Kargath{/link} want the magenta fungus caps that grow in its tunnels. On the {link:horde}Horde{/link}'s side, the sages of {link:thunder-bluff}Thunder Bluff{/link} and a salvage company of {link:orgrimmar}Orgrimmar{/link} have their own interests in the vault. Traders in {link:thousand-needles}Thousand Needles{/link} have learned of indurium, a rare ore of the dig that resists great heat.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "Within Uldaman",
			text = [=[• {b}The Outer Excavation.{/b} The dwarves' old works before the vault: the diggers' common halls, the Shadowforge camps, and the tunnels where the troggs first rose.
• {b}The Hall of the Keepers.{/b} A great temple hall split into long corridors, where the remains of a fallen paladin lie and the {link:troggs}trogg{/link} {link:revelosh}Revelosh{/link} hoards what he finds.
• {b}The {link:dwarves}Dwarves{/link}' Camp.{/b} A dig deep inside the vault where {link:the-lost-dwarves}Baelog{/link} and his band hold out against the troggs.
• {b}The Map Room.{/b} A chamber of titan devices. Beyond it lies the sealed Chamber of Khaz'mul, where the watcher {link:ironaya}Ironaya{/link} sleeps.
• {b}The Cavern of the Sentinel.{/b} A cave where the {link:obsidian-sentinel}Obsidian Sentinel{/link}, a guardian of black glass, stands watch.
• {b}The Temple Hall.{/b} A hall guarded by the {link:ancient-stone-keeper}Ancient Stone Keeper{/link}.
• {b}The Shadowforge Dig.{/b} An excavation where {link:galgann-firehammer}Galgann Firehammer{/link} drives the Dark Iron diggers.
• {b}The Stone Vault.{/b} The lair of {link:grimlok}Grimlok{/link} and his basilisk.
• {b}The Scorpid Cave.{/b} A side cavern full of scorpids, where a human enchanter named {link:annora}Annora{/link} hides.
• {b}The Hall of {link:archaedas}Archaedas{/link}.{/b} The deepest hall, lined with sleeping statues. Behind it lies the treasure chamber of the {link:lore-keeper-of-norgannon}Discs of Norgannon{/link}.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "The Record of the Discs",
			text = [=[According to the discs, the earthen were one of the first peoples the Creators placed on {link:azeroth}Azeroth{/link}. They were made to shape the deep places of the world, and they were meant to endure long after their makers had gone. Where the Creators usually built living beings from flesh, they made the earthen from the stone of the world itself, and so gave them great strength and a hide almost impervious to harm.

The earthen had one flaw. Under great strain, the pattern of their making could come undone. The {link:lore-keeper-of-norgannon}keeper of the discs{/link} cannot say what kind of strain; that knowledge, it says, is kept in another repository. The flaw was found late, and the Creators chose to amend the earthen rather than unmake them.

When the pattern failed, one of two peoples came of it. The first were the troggs, as strong as the earthen but nearly without thought, driven only by will and cunning. They breed as flesh does and eat stone and anything else they can take, even their own kind. The Creators judged them unacceptable. The second were the dwarves. They kept the strength of the earthen and their bond with the stone of the world, and some of them exceeded the earthen in wisdom. But they became creatures of flesh, who eat and bear children like any other living people. In plain words, the earthen could be cursed with flesh. Yet the Creators judged the dwarves worthy to exist in their own right.

The keeper cannot relate its record to the present age. Whether the dwarves and troggs of today are the peoples it describes, it will not say. In {link:ironforge}Ironforge{/link}, few now doubt it.]=],
		},
	},
})

